          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Copy Marker",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Values duplicated bitwise on assignment",
            "Source stays valid and usable afterwards",
            "`Copy: Clone` — Copy is a subtrait, derive both"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "#[derive(Clone, Copy)]",
            "struct P { x: i32, y: i32 }",
            "let a = 5; let b = a; // i32 is Copy",
            "println!(\"{a} {b}\"); // both still live"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Who Is Copy"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Copy",
            "Not Copy"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`i8`..`u128`, `usize`",
              "`String`"
            ],
            [
              "`f32`, `f64`, `bool`, `char`",
              "`Vec<T>`, `Box<T>`"
            ],
            [
              "`&T`, raw pointers",
              "`&mut T`, `HashMap`"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Tuples and arrays are Copy iff every element is",
            "`(i32, bool)` is Copy; `(i32, String)` is not",
            "Anything owning heap or a resource is out",
            "`&mut T` is not Copy — that would alias mutably"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Drop And Copy Never Mix",
          "text": "One type cannot have both — E0184. Duplicating a value that owns a destructor would double-drop it."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Semantics First",
          "text": "Ask: is duplicating this value meaningful? If it owns a resource, the answer is no."
        }
      ]
    },
